                    {
                        "sense_id": "63956f6e-4911-320a-b8bc-4bb023b8f1d9",
                        "entry_id": "af135bd2-0e02-3c15-997e-36236b9053e6",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A sex-linked mutation in mice that causes severe hypomyelination in males."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "45a2abcb-7d5c-4403-9f4e-38b578c48e7b",
                                "sense_id": "63956f6e-4911-320a-b8bc-4bb023b8f1d9",
                                "text": "When cerebellar tissues from normal and Jimpy are grown on the same slide in a common medium, no mutual effects can be observed: the Jimpy does not myelinate, and the normal does not stop myelination."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9b90e7c1-b957-4bcb-a405-d1efd11bd2a7",
                                "sense_id": "63956f6e-4911-320a-b8bc-4bb023b8f1d9",
                                "text": "1999, Klaus-Armin Nave, Chapter 2: X-Linked Dysmyelination: Mouse Models of Pelizaeus-Merzbacher Disease, Brian Popko (editor), Mouse Models in the Study of Genetic Neurological Disorders, Kluwer Academic / Plenum Publishers, page 29,\nThe jimpy phenotype is marked by a CNS-specific dysmyelination and has provided an authentic animal model for PMD (Sidman et al., 1964). Typically, 50% of the male progeny of jimpy carrier females are affected, whereas the heterozygous carrier female itself is unaffected."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
